Example 1 — Static in main(), calling main()
c#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    static int s = 5;
    printf("%d", s);
    if(--s)
        main();
    printf("%d", s);
    return 0;
}
O/P: 5 4 3 2 1 0 0 0 0 0 0
Trace:
s=5 → print 5 → s=4 (truthy) → main()
s=4 → print 4 → s=3 → main()
s=3 → print 3 → s=2 → main()
s=2 → print 2 → s=1 → main()
s=1 → print 1 → s=0 → (falsy, no recursion)
→ unwind: print 0,0,0,0,0 (s is static=0 for all)

Example 2 — Non-static in main(), calling main() → Stack Overflow
cint main() {
    int s = 5;
    printf("%d", s);
    if(--s)
        main();
    printf("%d", s);
    return 0;
}
O/P: 5 will be printed until RTE (Runtime Error) — Stack Overflow

Each main() call creates a new s=5 on stack → --s is always 4 (truthy) → infinite recursion → Stack Overflow.
